LOADCDA(8)           UNIX Programmer's Manual           LOADCDA(8)



NAME
     loadcda - install a classic desk accessory

SYNOPSIS
     loadcda file

DESCRIPTION
     Loadcda installs the Apple IIgs classic desk accessory (a
     ProDOS file of type $B9) in file, so that it appears in the
     Ctrl-OA-Esc menu until the machine restarts.  It reads the
     file's OMF load segments, reserves one block for them
     through /dev/cda, relocates each segment for its place in
     that block, copies it in, and has the Desk Manager install
     it.  If loadcda stops before the last step the block is
     given back.

     Loadcda also hands the module the file's absolute pathname
     and its resource fork, which it reads from ._file beside
     file, the AppleDouble file mount_prodos(8) presents for a
     forked ProDOS file.  While the desk accessory runs, the
     module answers the System Loader's LGetPathname2 with that
     pathname and serves the fork to GS/OS Open, SetMark, Read
     and Close from memory, and the GS/OS flag at $E1/00BC reads
     1.  A file with no ._file gets the pathname alone.

     Only the super-user can open /dev/cda.  The cda module must
     be loaded; /etc/rc loads it.  No desk accessory is installed
     at boot: each holds its segments and resource fork in memory
     from its loadcda until the machine restarts, because none of
     it can be read from disk while the Ctrl-OA-Esc menu runs, so
     one is installed when it is wanted, with loadcda.

     A desk accessory's first load segment must begin with its
     header.  The ~ExpressLoad segment is skipped.  Loadcda
     refuses a file that is not OMF version 2, a dynamic segment,
     a segment that is neither code nor data or has an absolute
     origin, desk accessories whose segments together need more
     than one bank, and the SUPER INTERSEG relocation records.

     Loadcda is silent when it succeeds.

FILES
     /dev/cda       the cda module's device
     ._file         file's resource fork, if it has one
     /usr/fgx/cda   the desk accessories shipped with the system

SEE ALSO
     mount_prodos(8), modload(8)

BUGS
     A desk accessory stays installed until the machine restarts;
     there is no way to remove one.

     The only GS/OS a desk accessory finds is its own resource
     fork, opened for reading: every other GS/OS call returns
     $0001, and every Loader function but LGetPathname2 returns
     $0002.  SetMark accepts only a position from the start of
     the fork, and a fork and pathname together must fit one
     bank.

     Loadcda installs at most eight desk accessories.  A UFS name
     holds 14 characters, so a desk accessory whose name is longer
     than 12 cannot have its ._file beside it there.



